      "text": "To find the period of the function \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\), we need to determine the smallest positive value \\( T \\) such that \\(\\tan(x + T) + \\cot(x + T) = \\tan x + \\cot x\\).\n\nFirst, recall the periodic properties of trigonometric functions:\n- The tangent function, \\(\\tan x\\), has a period of \\(\\pi\\). This means \\(\\tan(x + \\pi) = \\tan x\\).\n- The cotangent function, \\(\\cot x\\), also has a period of \\(\\pi\\). This means \\(\\cot(x + \\pi) = \\cot x\\).\n\nTherefore, for \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\) to be periodic with period \\( T \\), we need:\n\\[ \\tan(x + T) + \\cot(x + T) = \\tan x + \\cot x. \\]\n\nGiven the periodic properties:\n\\[ \\tan(x + \\pi) + \\cot(x + \\pi) = \\tan x + \\cot x. \\]\n\nSo, the smallest positive value \\( T \\) that satisfies this equation is \\( T = \\pi \\).\n\nThus, the period of \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\) is \\(\\pi\\).\n\nLet's verify this using Python to ensure the accuracy. We will check the periodicity by computing the values of \\( y \\) at different intervals and comparing them.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Define the function y = tan(x) + cot(x)\ndef y(x):\n    return np.tan(x) + 1/np.tan(x)\n\n# Check the value of y at several points\nx_values = np.linspace(0, 2*np.pi, 1000)\ny_values = y(x_values)\n\n# Calculate the period by finding the smallest positive T such that y(x + T) == y(x)\ny_values_1 = y_values\nT = np.pi  # Hypothetical period we are checking\n\n# Check if y(x + T) == y(x)\nare_equal = np.allclose(y_values_1, y(x_values + T))\n\nprint(are_equal)\n```\n```output\nTrue\n```\nThe Python computations confirm that the function \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\) indeed has a period of \\(\\pi\\).\n\nThus, the period of \\( \\boxed{\\pi} \\).",
