      "text": "To find the period of the function \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\), we first need to understand the periodicity of individual trigonometric functions involved, and then consider their periodicity in combination.\n\n1. **Period of \\(\\tan x\\):**\n   The tangent function \\( \\tan x \\) has a basic period of \\( \\pi \\). However, it can also be periodic for angles that are half-integer multiples of \\(\\pi\\). Thus, it repeats every \\( \\pi \\) or any number of \\(\\pi\\).\n\n2. **Period of \\(\\cot x\\):**\n   The cotangent function \\( \\cot x \\) also has a basic period of \\(\\pi\\), like the tangent function. This arises because the reciprocal trigonometric functions periodicity essentially reverses the tangent function.\n\nCombining both functions:\n\\[\ny = \\tan x + \\cot x\n\\]\n\nBoth \\(\\tan x\\) and \\(\\cot x\\) repeat at the same intervals due to their basic periodicity. Their combined period is the least common multiple (LCM) of the individual periods for their combined behavior over all angles.\n\nLet's calculate the LCM for these periods geometrically:\n\n- Period of \\(\\tan x\\) and \\(\\cot x\\) function is \\(\\pi\\).\n\nNow considering these intervals together for combining their effects:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\pi}\n\\]\n\nThis shows that the argument used for the combination of trigonometric functions representing \\(y =\\tan x + \\cot x\\) has a combined effectiveperiod of \\(\\pi\\). \n\nThus lastly,\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\pi}\n\\]",
