      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "Graph {eq}y = \\tan x + \\cot x.\n\n{/eq}\n\nAnswer:\n\nTo find the period of the function \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\), we need to analyze the behavior of the function and determine the smallest positive value \\( T \\) such that \\( y(x) = y(x + T) \\) for all \\( x \\).\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the given function in a more convenient form:\n\\[ y = \\tan x + \\cot x. \\]\n\nRecall the definitions of the tangent and cotangent functions:\n\\[ \\tan x = \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\cot x = \\frac{\\cos x}{\\sin x}. \\]\n\nSubstitute these into the function:\n\\[ y = \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x} + \\frac{\\cos x}{\\sin x}. \\]\n\nTo combine these fractions, find a common denominator:\n\\[ y = \\frac{\\sin^2 x + \\cos^2 x}{\\sin x \\cos x}. \\]\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity \\(\\sin^2 x + \\cos^2 x = 1\\), we get:\n\\[ y = \\frac{1}{\\sin x \\cos x}. \\]\n\nNext, use the double-angle identity for sine:\n\\[ \\sin x \\cos x = \\frac{1}{2} \\sin 2x. \\]\n\nSubstitute this identity into the function:\n\\[ y = \\frac{1}{\\frac{1}{2} \\sin 2x} = \\frac{2}{\\sin 2x}. \\]\n\nNow, observe the periodicity of \\( \\sin 2x \\):\n\\[ \\sin 2(x + T) = \\sin 2x \\text{ for all } x \\text{ and } T. \\]\n\nThus,\n\\[ \\sin 2(x + T) = \\sin 2x \\implies 2(x + T) = 2x + 2k\\pi \\text{ for some integer } k, \\]\n\\[ 2x + 2T = 2x + 2k\\pi. \\]\n\nBy canceling \\( 2x \\) from both sides, we get:\n\\[ 2T = 2k\\pi \\implies T = k\\pi. \\]\n\nSince \\( T \\) must be the smallest positive value, we take \\( k = 1 \\):\n\\[ T = \\pi. \\]\n\nTherefore, the period of the function \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{\\pi}. \\]",
